  "content": {
    "title": "How Luke Murray to BC impacts UConn men's basketball",
    "text": "UConn head coach Dan Hurley, left, talks with assistant coach Luke Murray during the first half of an exhibition NCAA basketball game against Boston College, Monday, Oct. 13, 2025, in Uncasville, Conn. (AP Photo/Jessica Hill)Jessica Hill/Associated PressNow that we know that Luke Murray will be taking over the head coaching job at Boston College, questions abound about how it could affect the UConn men's basketball program. And they have nothing to do with whether Bill Murray will now switch his allegiances from UConn to BC (though that may be a burning issue for Husky fans).Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Here are a few questions surrounding Luke Murray's departure for his first head coaching job:Will he remain with UConn until the season ends?Murray is with the team in Washington, D.C. and has been \"laser-focused\" all week, according to a locker room source. Dan Hurley said Murray has the game prep duties for Duke in case the Huskies play them on Sunday, so he'll certainly be around at least until then.If the Huskies win their two games in D.C. and advance to the Final Four, will Murray accompany them to Indianapolis? No definitive answer from UConn yet. One spokesman said there's \"no indication he will depart early,\" but nothing is set in stone.Article continues below this adMore For YouCT Insider LogoMake CT Insider a preferred source on Google to see more of our journalism when you search.Add Preferred SourceIt would seem likely that Murray would remain for what would be one final weekend with the Huskies. He could take care of some of his Boston College duties early in the week before going full-bore into whomever UConn would face in the Final Four. We see similar instances in college football all the time, where a departing assistant coach stays on with the team he's leaving through their bowl games.Has the BC situation affected UConn's preparation this week?A resounding \"No\" from all parties on that one.Article continues below this adHurley noted on Thursday that the program was handling Murray's situation \"easily.\"\"We're real transparent with each other,\" Hurley said. \"Obviously, the timeline of these hiring processes, there's no portal for hiring. So, it's not a problem.\"His players agreed.\"I don't think it really matters right now,\" Malachi Smith said. \"We're just trying to finish the last couple of weeks, and whatever he does, we're going to respect his decision. I'd be happy if he stays, and I'd be happy if he goes.\"Article continues below this ad\"I think we're all doing a great job staying locked-in on this game, what this game means for us this year,\" added Jayden Ross. \"Coach Hurley said it, 'There's no going back after this. This is the last team that this team will play again.' I think we're doing a great job staying locked-in on that.\"What will UConn miss most about Murray?Murray was the coaching staff's analytics guy and certainly helped Hurley adopt a very analytical approach, as well. He also famously helped institute UConn's complex, highly-successful offensive system, a huge key in their back-to-back national championships in 2023 and 2024. Murray had a lifelong love of the recruiting process and helped bring in UConn standouts like Donovan Clingan, Liam McNeeley and Braylon Mullins. He was also instrumental in nabbing Cam Spencer and others out of the transfer portal.Article continues below this adWill this hurt UConn in recruiting?Probably not. UConn has used an analytics company, HD Intelligence, to help narrow in on potential transfer recruits, as well as what opponents to schedule, so it should still do well in the portal. And Hurley takes a lot of pride in finding the right kind of high school talent that will fit his program. While Murray will likely be mining some of the same waters for recruits, it doesn't seem overly likely that too many top prospects will choose BC over UConn, though NIL budgets could be a big factor.Who replaces Murray on the UConn bench?Maintaining their ultra-effective offense shouldn't be a big issue, but the Huskies will almost certainly look to an analytics-oriented assistant to replace Murray. It's hard to know which way they'd go right now.Article continues below this adAndrew Hurley, Dan's son, is graduating with an MBA this spring and wants to stay in the college game. He'd prefer to become a general manager and has learned a lot under current GM Tom Moore, who clearly preferred coaching to his current role. Would Moore slide on back over to an assistant coach's role and have Andrew take over as GM? Andrew's only 24, so it may be a bit soon for that.First-year assistant Mike Nardi will likely assume a bigger role, and perhaps a younger coach like Andrew takes the open spot. Or Dan Hurley looks for someone more experienced. Taliek Brown back over from St. John's? Unlikely. Another current UConn grad assistant, like Shelton's Rich Kelly? Kelly greatly enjoyed his one season as a player at BC, so maybe he follows Murray to Chestnut Hill?More likely, Hurley finds an assistant with a little more experience. Which leads to the $64,000 question ...Will Bobby Hurley join his brother's staff?This appears to be an unequivocal \"No.\" Bobby, whose 11-year tenure as Arizona State's head coach has come to an end, served as Dan's assistant both at Wagner and Rhode Island. But he recently told CBSSports.com that he has no interest in joining his brother at UConn. In fact, he said that if he can't find a head coaching job (and there are plenty of good ones out there right now), he'd probably get into TV for a while until a good opportunity pops up. Dan Hurley also told CT Insider that he believes Bobby will \"land on his feet\" as a head coach somewhere.Article continues below this adOh, and about Bill Murray's new allegiances ...Nothing official here, of course. But when you consider that Bill was a big Xavier fan, showing up to numerous games, when Luke was an assistant there, and a big Louisville fan when Luke was an assistant there, and even showed up to several URI games ... yes, it seems pretty apparent that Bill will be sporting maroon-and-gold next season — a painful thought for UConn fans, no doubt.CT Insider LogoGet daily updates from the UConn basketball world.March 27, 2026Staff WriterDave Borges is a sports reporter for Hearst Connecticut Media Group covering the UConn men's basketball team. An award-winning journalist, Dave has covered the Huskies since 2007, including coverage of five Final Fours and four national championships (2011, 2014, 2023 and 2024). He has been an Associated Press Top 25 poll voter since 2009 and was named NSMA Connecticut Sportswriter of the Year in 2019. Dave has authored three books: \"Hurley's Heroes: UConn's Return to College Basketball's Elite,\" \"Rebound: The Incredible Story of UConn Basketball's Comeback from Defeat to Dominance,\" and \"Images of Baseball: The Pawtucket Red Sox.\" He also covers baseball and was a beat writer chronicling the 2004 World Series-champion Boston Red Sox. Dave has been a Baseball Hall of Fame voter since 2014. He can be reached at david.borges@hearstmediact.com.",
